    <!-- Article body -->
    <section class="py-16 px-4">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto prose prose-lg prose-gray">

        <p class="lead text-xl text-gray-700 font-medium">
          Customs tariffs, supply pressure, supplier risk, financing costs, political uncertainty… In New Caledonia as across the Pacific, these external signals now arrive faster than strategic planning cycles. McKinsey Quarterly identified five levers that turn this constraint into a competitive advantage. Here is the PerfEco reading of them, adapted to the complex organisations of our region.
        </p>

        <h2>1. Instability has become structural — stop waiting for it to pass</h2>
        <p>
          The first reflex in the face of uncertainty is to wait. To hope the context settles before deciding. That is understandable. It is also a strategic mistake.
        </p>
        <p>
          McKinsey puts it plainly: the companies that outperform through volatile periods are not the ones that predicted the future. They are the ones that <strong>accepted instability as a given</strong> and organised their management around it.
        </p>
        <p>
          For an executive committee in New Caledonia, that means designing decision-making that works <em>inside</em> uncertainty — not after it.
        </p>

        <h2>2. The real risk is not volatility — it is the paralysis it causes</h2>
        <p>
          When the environment turns unstable, organisations tend to slow down: decisions get postponed, projects suspended, trade-offs left vague. Every team waits for the next one. Inaction settles in.
        </p>
        <p>
          The phenomenon is well documented: <strong>waiting itself becomes an operational risk</strong>. Suppliers are not challenged, investments are frozen, talented people lose motivation. The organisation loses responsiveness exactly when it needs it most.
        </p>

        <h2>3. What an executive committee should clarify first</h2>
        <p>McKinsey identifies four essential areas to clarify:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Critical processes</strong> — what absolutely has to keep working, whatever happens.</li>
          <li><strong>Sensitive suppliers</strong> — the ones whose failure would have a major impact on the business.</li>
          <li><strong>Useful metrics</strong> — not more dashboards, but metrics that genuinely trigger a decision.</li>
          <li><strong>Decision rights</strong> — who settles what, when, and on what information.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Clarity today equals responsiveness tomorrow. The equation is simple. It is rarely acted on before a crisis makes it urgent.
        </p>

        <h2>4. Do not add complexity to uncertainty</h2>
        <p>
          The classic mistake in turbulent times: setting up crisis committees, multiplying follow-up meetings, making reporting heavier. The answer to uncertainty is not one more strategic plan.
        </p>
        <p>It is usually the opposite:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Simplify decision routes</li>
          <li>Make management data trustworthy</li>
          <li>Prioritise short projects with measurable impact</li>
          <li>Prepare courses of action (not forecasts)</li>
          <li>Make trade-offs visible to the whole leadership team</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Performance is won in the <strong>quality of execution</strong> — not in the sophistication of the plan.
        </p>

        <h2>5. Steering through uncertainty means organising clarity</h2>
        <p>
          The fifth McKinsey lever may be the most counter-intuitive: in a volatile world, the priority is not to have more information. It is to have the <em>right</em> information, shared with the <em>right</em> people, at the <em>right</em> time.
        </p>
        <p>
          Organisations that succeed in steering through uncertainty all have one thing in common: they worked on internal clarity before working on external strategy. They know what matters, who decides, and how information travels up.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-teal rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="text-gray-700 font-medium mb-0">
            <strong>Does your executive committee know which trade-offs to prepare before the next shock?</strong><br/>
            PerfEco works with the leaders of complex organisations to bring strategy and execution closer together, clarify processes, make management data trustworthy, and smooth coordination between departments.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
